James inquired about academic papers focusing on energy system models, specifically integrated assessment models (IAMs) optimized under uncertainty to evaluate costs of emission reductions necessary for limiting global warming to below 2°C. I confirmed the existence of several relevant studies that utilize stochastic optimization, robust optimization, and multi-stage decision-making frameworks to navigate uncertainties in technology costs, policy changes, and climate responses. Key papers mentioned include: **"The Economics of 1.5°C Climate Change,"** **"Stochastic Integrated Assessment of Climate Change Inducing Catastrophic Damages,"** **"Robust Climate Policies Under Uncertainty,"** **"The Climate Uncertainty Genie,"** and **"Assessing the Macroeconomic Impacts and the Value of Earlier Mitigation."** These works emphasize the necessity for adaptable policy design to effectively manage risks associated with climate change mitigation strategies.
